{
  "id": "physics-004",
  "title": "Why do we see lightning before we hear thunder?",
  "category": "physics",
  "subject_area": "waves and sound",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "How can the gap between a flash of lightning and the sound of thunder tell you how far away the storm is?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "The flash from lightning reaches you almost instantly, but thunder is sound, so the delay between flash and thunder grows with the distance to the strike.",
  "domain_of_validity": "Lightning within hearing range, roughly 10 miles; the speed of sound changes a little with air temperature.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "Lightning heats the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius in a split second. The air bursts outward and makes a sound wave, which we hear as thunder. You see the flash first, because sound travels only about one mile every five seconds. Count the seconds between the flash and the thunder, divide by five, and you get the distance to the lightning in miles.",
    "level_2": "Thunder is the shock wave made when lightning heats the air so fast that it expands explosively. Millions of small shock waves along the path of the bolt reach you at slightly different times, which is why thunder rumbles. Sound moves roughly 750 miles per hour, about one mile every five seconds, and it moves faster in warm air than in cool air. Dividing the delay in seconds by five gives the distance in miles.",
    "level_3": "The light from the flash arrives almost instantly, so the delay t between flash and thunder is the time sound needs to cover the distance d. This gives d = v t, where v is the speed of sound in air, which depends on temperature. With v of about 1,200 kilometres per hour, which is roughly 330 metres per second, every 3 seconds of delay is about 1 kilometre."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "Counting the seconds from flash to thunder and dividing by five gives the distance to the strike in miles. Fifteen seconds means about three miles.",
    "Thunder from a close strike is a sharp crack, while thunder from a distant strike is a continuous rumble."
  ],
